<p>A forthcoming study shows that a critical thinking course focused on a few good, relatively easy to learn, and useful reasoning strategies can impart lessons that remain effective long after the course has ended.<span id="more-56580"></span></p>
<p>The <a href="https://philpapers.org/rec/BISCTC" target="_blank" rel="noopener">study</a>, authored by <a href="https://philosophy.fsu.edu/person/michael-bishop" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Michael Bishop</a> (Florida State), <a href="https://www.ou.edu/cas/psychology/people/faculty/adam-feltz" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adam Feltz</a> (Oklahoma), and <a href="https://www.paulconway.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paul Conway</a> (Southampton), will be coming out in <a href="https://www.apa.org/pubs/journals/xap" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>The Journal of Experimental Psychology: Applied</em></a>.</p>
<p>In the following guest post, Professor Bishop describes the course, the motivation for it, the design of the study, and the results.</p>
<p>An appendix at the end of the post contains the study&#8217;s questions.</p>
<hr />
<h2><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="wp-image-56592 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/critical-thinking-impression.jpg" alt="" width="773" height="425" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/critical-thinking-impression.jpg 1000w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/critical-thinking-impression-300x165.jpg 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/critical-thinking-impression-768x422.jpg 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/critical-thinking-impression-400x220.jpg 400w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 773px) 100vw, 773px" /></h2>
<h2 style="text-align: center;">Crafting a Critical Thinking Course that Sticks with Students<br />
<em>by Michael Bishop</em></h2>
<p>Two large longitudinal studies conducted by Adam Feltz, Paul Conway, and me suggest that a critical thinking class can promote large and enduring improvements in how students think about important everyday issues. These studies are described in an <a href="https://philpapers.org/rec/BISCTC">article</a> forthcoming in <em>The Journal of Experimental Psychology: Applied</em>.</p>
<h3>Background</h3>
<p>In <a href="https://academic.oup.com/book/36404?login=false"><em>Epistemology and the Psychology of Human Judgment</em></a> (2005), JD Trout and I defended the view that good reasoning strategies are reliable, cheap (i.e., easy to use), and useful. We argued that this view, <a href="https://compass.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1747-9991.2008.00161.x">Strategic Reliabilism</a>, provides a powerful framework for evaluating and repairing our epistemic practices.</p>
<p>To prove the ameliorative power of Strategic Reliabilism, I revised my critical thinking class. This turned out to be a long-term project. (Thanks, tenure!) I stopped teaching reasoning strategies that were expensive (e.g., expected utility, rules of logic and probability) or of dubious utility (e.g., informal fallacies). And I started teaching good, cheap, and useful reasoning strategies. For the purposes of a critical thinking class, I took good reasoning strategies to be those that tend to produce accurate judgments (for theoretical reasoning) or favorable outcomes given the reasoner’s values (for practical reasoning). The class teaches students to use reasoning strategies for sampling arguments, testimony, the law of large numbers, herd reasoning, overconfidence, mental accounting, confirmation biases, and status quo biases. Here are five examples of good, cheap, and useful reasoning strategies I teach, each with its own rhyming ditty.</p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><strong>Causal Rule</strong>: Don’t infer causation from correlation. <em>Causal arguments have holes if they don’t have controls.</em></p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><strong>Regression Rule</strong>: For events that have a central tendency, recognize that extreme events tend to be followed by more average or typical events. <em>If something impresses, it probably regresses.</em></p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><strong>Sunk Cost Rule</strong>: Unrecoverable costs cannot be a consideration that favors one course of action over another. <em>Wasted money and time will vex if you let ‘em, but when making decisions it’s best to forget ‘em.</em></p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><strong>Opportunity Cost Rule</strong>: When reflecting on a course of action, recognize the cost of not pursuing the best alternative. <em>If an option’s imperative, you have reasons comparative. </em></p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><strong>Gambler’s Fallacy Rule</strong>: Treat statistically independent events as statistically independent. <em>You should guess runs regress but doubt they even out.</em></p>
<p>Do students learn to think better when they learn to use good, cheap, and useful reasoning strategies?</p>
<h3>Study Design</h3>
<p>113 critical thinking students (the training group) and 284 students in other introductory philosophy classes (the control group) took a survey at the start and end of the semester. The survey asked them to evaluate 5 instances of reasoning, each of which violated one of the 5 rules described above. We waited 16 months, and we gave them a survey that asked them to evaluate <em>different</em> instances of reasoning, each of which violated one of the 5 rules.</p>
<p>The surveys included two<em> skepticism checks</em>, reasoning that did not violate any rules taught during training. This was to assess whether training had simply taught students to be hyper-critical. (See Appendix for all 14 instances of reasoning the students evaluated.)</p>
<h3>Results</h3>
<p>Training improved reasoning on 4 of the 5 rules, with the gambler’s fallacy being the lone exception. On the pre-test, both groups positively evaluated reasoning that violated the causal rule, the regression rule, the sunk cost rule, and the opportunity cost rule. On the post-test and 16-month delayed test, the training group was significantly more critical. These improvements were large. Averaging across all 4 items, on the post-test, the training group’s mean was about 1.4 standard deviations better (i.e., more critical) than the control group’s mean. And it was almost 1 standard deviation better on the 16-month delayed test.</p>
<p>On the pre-test, both groups evaluated the skepticism checks positively. On the post-test and 16-month delayed test, the training group was somewhat more critical than the control group, although the difference was not statistically significant. Did training promote unwarranted skepticism? Or wariness of reasoning proposed by their critical thinking teacher? Or a healthy dose of epistemic humility? This issue requires more attention. (See Appendix for more detailed results.)</p>
<h3>3-Month Study</h3>
<p>The previous year, we conducted a similar study with 395 students and two delayed tests. The first occurred 3 months after the class ended and was identical to the pre- and post-tests. The second occurred two weeks later and included all new reasoning items. The results were similar to the results of the 16-month study. On the post-test and delayed tests, the training group showed large improvements on 4 of the 5 rules, with the gambler’s fallacy being the exception. The response rates on the 3-month delayed tests were 39.2% and 32.9%, whereas the response rate on the 16-month delayed test was 23.7%. We speculate that we lost more students to graduation on the 16-month study.</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">*  *  *</p>
<h3>Appendix: 16-Month Study Details</h3>
<p>On each survey, students were given 7 instances of reasoning (5 rule-violation items and 2 non-rule violation items). They were asked to accept the premises and then evaluate on a 7-point scale whether the premises give us “no reason” (1), “moderate reason” (4), or “very strong reason” (7) to accept the conclusion. The bar graphs (with the lowest possible “no reason” response as the zero point) give the mean rating on each question. (Grey = control group. Black = training group.) All measures, datasets, and analyses for the 3-month and 16-month studies are on this project’s OSF page: <a href="https://osf.io/syxr9/">https://osf.io/syxr9/</a>.</p>
<h4>Causal Rule</h4>
<p><em>Pre &amp; Post Tests</em>: The Anti-Crime Law reformed Moldova&#8217;s social and judicial policies. The goal of the law was to reduce crime. In the two years following the enactment of the law, the crime rate in Moldova dropped by 3%. Conclusion: The Moldovan Anti-Crime Law was an effective deterrent: It reduced crime.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-56581 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-1.png" alt="" width="573" height="395" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-1.png 573w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-1-300x207.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-1-400x276.png 400w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 573px) 100vw, 573px" /></p>
<p><em>16-Month Delayed Test</em>: The state of Caledonia puts in place new water pollution regulations. One year later, water pollution levels in Caledonia are unchanged. The lakes and streams of Caledonia are just as polluted as they were when the new regulations were put in place. Conclusion: The new water pollution regulations had <u>no effect</u> on Caledonia&#8217;s levels of water pollution.</p>
<h4>Regression Rule</h4>
<p><em>Pre &amp; Post Tests</em>: Sam is a good baseball player. But sometimes he has a bad game. After he has a bad game, Sam does extra mental exercises before his next game. And his performance almost always improves. Conclusion<strong>:</strong> Sam&#8217;s extra mental exercises have a good effect on his performance.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-56582 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-2.png" alt="" width="604" height="411" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-2.png 604w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-2-300x204.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-2-400x272.png 400w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 604px) 100vw, 604px" /></p>
<p><em>16-Month Delayed Test</em>: Pat is a swim coach. Pat rewards swimmers who have a great race with a &#8220;Great Race&#8221; award. After swimmers receive a “Great Race” award, they usually swim slower in their next race. Conclusion: Pat’s “Great Race” award tends to have a <u>negative effect</u> on swimmers&#8217; performance.</p>
<h4>Opportunity Cost Rule</h4>
<p><em>Pre &amp; Post Tests</em>: If you sign this contract with Corporation XYZ, your costs over the next year will be $50,000 more than your revenues. Conclusion: You should not sign this contract with Corporation XYZ.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-56583 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-3.png" alt="" width="608" height="411" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-3.png 608w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-3-300x203.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-3-400x270.png 400w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 608px) 100vw, 608px" /></p>
<p><em>16-Month Delayed Test</em>: The Cannington Corporation is deciding on an investment strategy for the next three years. If they adopt the “whiplash” strategy, their investment portfolio will grow by 8% each year for the next three years. Conclusion: The Cannington Corporation <u>should adopt</u> the “whiplash” investment strategy for the next three years.</p>
<h4>Sunk Cost Rule</h4>
<p><em>Pre &amp; Post Tests</em>: Sam is financially comfortable but not wealthy, and he takes a one-week vacation every year. Three months ago, he paid a $500 deposit to rent a beach house during his vacation. He now realizes that he would enjoy a week at a mountain resort far more than at the beach house. The $500 deposit on the beach house is non-refundable. Conclusion<strong>:</strong> The $500 deposit is a factor that weighs in favor of going to the beach.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-56584 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-4.png" alt="" width="605" height="410" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-4.png 605w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-4-300x203.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-4-400x271.png 400w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 605px) 100vw, 605px" /></p>
<p><em>16-Month Delayed Test</em>: Freedonia has been at war with its neighbor Sylvania for four years. They&#8217;re fighting over Pearl Island and its rich natural resources, which Sylvania currently controls. In this war, Freedonia has paid a very heavy price in terms of lost lives and money, and it must now decide whether to continue to fight or sign a peace treaty. If Freedonia continues to fight, it will eventually defeat Sylvania and gain control of Pearl Island. If Freedonia signs a peace treaty, Sylvania will stay in control of Pearl Island. Conclusion: The cost in lives and money Freedonia has paid over the past four years is a factor that weighs in favor of continuing the war.</p>
<h4>Gambler’s Fallacy Rule</h4>
<p><em>Pre &amp; Post Tests</em>: A coin is fair. That means that the coin has a tendency to come up heads half (50%) of the time and tails half (50%) of the time. So far, we&#8217;ve flipped the coin 20 times. By chance, it has comes up heads 15 times and tails 5 times (75% heads). We plan to flip the coin 80 more times (for a total of 100 flips). Conclusion: In the next 80 flips, the coin is likely to come up tails more often than heads.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-56585 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-5.png" alt="" width="612" height="410" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-5.png 612w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-5-300x201.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-5-400x268.png 400w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 612px) 100vw, 612px" /></p>
<p><em>16-Month Delayed Test</em>: A spinning wheel is fair. That means that on any spin, the wheel has a tendency to come up green half (50%) of the time, and red half (50%) of the time. So far, we&#8217;ve spun the wheel 20 times. By chance, it has comes up red 15 times (75% red). Conclusion: In the next series of spins, the wheel is likely to come up green more often than red until the proportion of green and red outcomes are closer to even (50-50).</p>
<h4>Skepticism Check (Causal Reasoning)</h4>
<p><em>Pre &amp; Post Tests</em>: The Anti-Crime Law reformed Moldova&#8217;s social and judicial policies. The goal of the law was to reduce crime. In the two years following the enactment of the law, the crime rate in Moldova dropped by 3%. In similar countries that did not pass such a law, the crime rate dropped by 1%. Conclusion: The Moldovan Anti-Crime Law was an effective deterrent: It reduced crime.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-56586 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-6.png" alt="" width="600" height="406" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-6.png 600w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-6-300x203.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-6-400x271.png 400w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 600px) 100vw, 600px" /></p>
<p><em>16-Month Delayed Test</em>: 23 cities, including Townville, imposed a ban on high cholesterol oils in restaurants and grocery stores. Since these bans were imposed five years ago, health measures (such as levels of heart disease, obesity, diabetes, and mortality) in those cities, including Townville, have remained constant. But over those same five years, in similar cities that have not banned high cholesterol oils, those health measures have gotten noticeably worse. Conclusion: The high cholesterol oil ban had a <u>positive effect</u> on the health of the Townville residents.</p>
<h4>Skepticism Check (Practical Reasoning)</h4>
<p><em>Pre &amp; Post Tests</em>: If you sign this contract with Corporation XYZ, your costs over the next year will be $50,000 more than your revenues. Your best alternative &#8211; signing a different contract with Denko Corporation &#8211; will result in a significant profit (your revenues will be considerably greater than your costs). Conclusion: You should not sign this contract with Corporation XYZ.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-56587 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-7.png" alt="" width="581" height="393" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-7.png 581w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-7-300x203.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/bishop-critical-thinking-7-400x271.png 400w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 581px) 100vw, 581px" /></p>
<p><em>16-Month Delayed Test</em>: Ecuador is deciding whether to implement a new health care policy. They&#8217;re considering the best three options: Plan A, Plan B, and Plan C. The three plans will cost about the same. (Health care costs will make up about 8.4% of the economy on all three plans.) But Plan A has far better health outcomes for the people of Ecuador than Plans B and C. Conclusion:  Ecuador should adopt Plan A.</p>
